  "prompt": "Which methods did Socrates employ to challenge the prevailing thoughts of his time?",
  "answer": "Socrates challenged the prevailing ideas of ancient Athens mainly through dialogue rather than lectures. His methods included:\n\n- **Probing questions:** He asked people to explain familiar concepts such as justice, courage, and virtue, then examined the assumptions behind their answers.\n- **Exposing contradictions (*elenchus*):** Through follow-up questions, he showed when someone’s beliefs conflicted with one another, prompting reconsideration.\n- **Professing ignorance:** He presented himself as someone seeking knowledge rather than possessing it. This “Socratic irony” encouraged others to explain—and reveal the limits of—their supposed expertise.\n- **Seeking clear definitions:** He pushed beyond individual examples to ask what made something just, good, or virtuous in general.\n- **Prioritizing reason over authority:** He questioned whether traditions, popular opinion, and respected leaders deserved acceptance simply because they were influential.\n\nHis aim was to encourage intellectual humility and an examined life, especially concerning how people ought to live. We know these methods chiefly through accounts by writers such as Plato and Xenophon; Socrates left no writings himself.",
